sections.s2_technical:
Interface-based projection defines an interface with getter methods. Spring Data generates a proxy at runtime that maps only the declared columns. The proxy intercepts method calls and returns the corresponding column value.

```java
// Interface projection — only tradeId + symbol fetched:
public interface TradeIdAndSymbol {
    String getTradeId();
    String getSymbol();
}

// Repository:
List<TradeIdAndSymbol> findByStatus(String status);
// Generates: SELECT t.trade_id, t.symbol FROM trades t WHERE t.status = ?
// NOT: SELECT t.trade_id, t.symbol, t.amount, t.account_id, t.settlement_date, ...

// Usage:
List<TradeIdAndSymbol> trades = tradeRepo.findByStatus("SETTLED");
trades.get(0).getTradeId();  // "TRD-001"
trades.get(0).getSymbol();   // "RELIANCE"
```

Class/DTO projection uses a JPQL constructor expression or a record as the return type. This is the MOST performant approach — no proxy, no entity management, direct object construction.

```java
// DTO record:
public record TradeOverview(String tradeId, String symbol, BigDecimal amount) {}

// Repository with JPQL constructor expression:
@Query("SELECT new com.bank.dto.TradeOverview(t.tradeId, t.symbol, t.amount) FROM Trade t WHERE t.status = :status")
List<TradeOverview> findOverviewByStatus(@Param("status") String status);
// Generates: SELECT t.trade_id, t.symbol, t.amount FROM trades t WHERE t.status = ?
// Result: List<TradeOverview> — plain records, no proxies, no persistence context entry

// Spring Data also supports record return type directly (Spring Data 3.x):
List<TradeOverview> findByStatus(String status);
// If TradeOverview has a constructor matching the entity fields, Spring maps automatically
```

Dynamic projection lets the caller choose the projection class at runtime.

```java
// Repository with dynamic projection:
<T> List<T> findByAccountId(String accountId, Class<T> type);

// Usage — caller picks what they want:
List<TradeIdAndSymbol> idsOnly = tradeRepo.findByAccountId("SAV2001", TradeIdAndSymbol.class);
List<TradeOverview> overviews = tradeRepo.findByAccountId("SAV2001", TradeOverview.class);
List<Trade> fullEntities = tradeRepo.findByAccountId("SAV2001", Trade.class);
// Same method, different projections!
```

Performance comparison. Full entity loads ALL columns and enters the persistence context (dirty checking, snapshots). Interface projection fetches only declared columns but creates a proxy object. DTO projection fetches only needed columns AND creates a plain object (no proxy, no persistence context). DTO is 20-40% faster than full entity for wide tables.

```java
// FULL ENTITY: SELECT * FROM trades (all 15 columns) + persistence context management
List<Trade> full = tradeRepo.findByStatus("SETTLED");

// INTERFACE: SELECT trade_id, symbol FROM trades (2 columns) + proxy overhead
List<TradeIdAndSymbol> iface = tradeRepo.findByStatus("SETTLED");

// DTO: SELECT trade_id, symbol, amount FROM trades (3 columns) + plain object
List<TradeOverview> dto = tradeRepo.findOverviewByStatus("SETTLED");

// Performance ranking (fastest to slowest):
// 1. DTO projection (fewest columns, no proxy, no persistence context)
// 2. Interface projection (few columns, but proxy overhead)
// 3. Full entity (all columns, persistence context, dirty checking)
```

